      "text": "To determine the number of rows in the can display, we need to understand the pattern in the number of cans in each row. The display starts with one can in the top row, and each subsequent row has two more cans than the previous row. This forms an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a_1\\) is 1 and the common difference \\(d\\) is 2.\n\nThe number of cans in the \\(n\\)-th row can be given by the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n\\[ a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d \\]\nSubstituting the known values \\(a_1 = 1\\) and \\(d = 2\\), we get:\n\\[ a_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 = 1 + 2n - 2 = 2n - 1 \\]\n\nThe total number of cans in the display is the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of this arithmetic sequence. The sum \\(S_n\\) of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (a_1 + a_n) \\]\nSubstituting the known values \\(a_1 = 1\\) and \\(a_n = 2n - 1\\), we get:\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (1 + (2n - 1)) = \\frac{n}{2} \\cdot 2n = n^2 \\]\n\nWe are given that the total number of cans is 100, so we set up the equation:\n\\[ n^2 = 100 \\]\nTo find \\(n\\), we take the square root of both sides:\n\\[ n = \\sqrt{100} = 10 \\]\n\nTherefore, the display contains \\(\\boxed{10}\\) rows.",
